<section class="page-intro">
  <p class="eyebrow">About Jane</p><h1>Good tools begin with respect for the work.</h1><p class="lede">My work connects hospitality operations, product strategy, and software. The starting point is always the same: understand what people need to get through a real day.</p>
</section>
<section class="editorial section accent-tiles">
  <h2>Hospitality is<br>a good teacher.</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Service depends on small details being understood and carried through. An order changes. A team hands over a shift. A guest needs something that does not fit the usual pattern.</p><p>Software should support that judgment. It should reduce friction without flattening the human experience or asking people to organize their work around the tool.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="editorial section">
  <h2>Ownership should<br>be practical.</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Through swiftlabs.xyz, I’m exploring what it takes for people and small businesses to keep control of their digital lives: usable data, an identity they can retain, and the freedom to change who provides or maintains their tools.</p><p>That also means planning for things to go wrong. Recovery should be understandable. Help from an agent should come with clear permissions and explanations. A useful tool should leave room for a person to take over.</p><p>These are design commitments and questions to test. The restaurant experiment is the first place I’m putting them to work.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/">See the current exploration <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
  </div>
</section>
